  "image_name": "e9fff75d018f6511835b294a846fe7a6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways of thinking about interfacial charge transfer between a metal and a tungsten oxide (WO) semiconductor, and shows how illumination changes the picture. On the left, the traditional Butler–Volmer model treats the process as motion over free-energy barriers: two colored reaction-coordinate curves connect metal states to WO states, with different barrier heights linked to the forward and reverse directions and a note indicating no entropy change.\n\nThe middle and right panels recast the same situation as a population model based on electronic energy levels. In the dark, electrons occupy states near the WO valence band, with dashed lines marking two relevant energies, and transfer is represented by upward and downward arrows between occupied populations and the interfacial barrier. The blue potential curve rises toward the WO side, indicating the energetic landscape carriers experience.\n\nUnder light, a new population level appears, labeled near \\(E_p\\), and yellow carriers are added, suggesting photoexcited holes or altered carrier populations. The conduction and valence bands are explicitly shown, and the arrows indicate that illumination changes the available transitions and populations rather than simply shifting a single activation barrier. Overall, the figure argues for a population-based description of dark and photoinduced interfacial transfer.",
